-- title:
--   Level automorphisms act on the Tate point by diamond relabelling
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a nonzero natural number $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$, and a prime $\ell_g$ with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$.
--
--   **Base field and root of unity.** $L$ is a field of characteristic zero, $\xi \in L$ is a primitive $q\ell_g$-th root of unity, and the hypothesis `hιξ` provides a ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota \xi = \exp(2\pi i/(q\ell_g))$.
--
--   **Level group and function field.** $H_1$ is a subgroup of $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ required by `hH₁` to be [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22) intersected with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell_g)^\times$; the first factor is itself the kernel of the reduction to $(\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, so $H_1$ consists of the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$ and modulo $\ell_g$. The intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq L((\mathsf q))$ is required by `hK` to be [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the $L$-subfield of $L((\mathsf q))$ generated by the image, under coefficientwise extension $\mathbb{Q} \to L$, of the function field of $X_{H_1}(q^2M')$ inside $\mathbb{Q}((\mathsf q))$.
--
--   **Base ring.** $A$ is a discrete valuation ring which is a domain, equipped with an algebra structure over which $L$ is its fraction field, with $q$ in the maximal ideal of $A$; $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $A \to L \to K$. Further, $\ell_g$ and $M'$ are units in $A$ (`hℓA`, `hM'A`). An element $j \in K$ is given whose Laurent expansion is the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), and $j \neq 0$.
--
--   **Variable-change compatibilities.** Three hypotheses are the data entering `rigidDataH1Pow`: `hℓ` asserts that for every $A$-algebra $T$, every Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$, every variable change $C$ and every quadruple $D = (x_P,y_P,x_Q,y_Q)$, the predicate [`ModularCurve.IsGamma1Point W ℓg D`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma1Pow.html#L10) (the affine equation holds at $(x_P,y_P)$, the $\ell_g$-division polynomial `preΨ` vanishes at $x_P$, and $x_Q = x_P$, $y_Q = y_P$) is preserved by passing to $C \bullet W$ and $D$ transformed by $C$; `hM` asserts that [`ModularCurve.IsGamma0PowAt W p k h`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma0Pow.html#L55) is preserved by $C$ together with the substitution `kernelVariableChangeDeg C (gamma0PowDeg p k)` on the kernel polynomial $h$; `hL` asserts that divisibility $h \mid$ `inLineMulPoly W ℓg n x` is transported to `kernelVariableChangeDeg C d h` $\mid$ `inLineMulPoly (C • W) ℓg n` at the transformed abscissa $u^{-2}(x - r)$.
--
--   **Group laws, level transport and graded models.** $\mathcal G$ is a family of relative group laws on the projective models of Weierstrass curves with invertible discriminant over $A$-algebras, assumed chord–tangent (`h𝒢`: it is computed by a points evaluation) and origin-normalised (`h𝒢O`: the identity section is cut out by a chart homomorphism killing $x/y$ and $z/y$); $\mathcal T$ is a level transport for $\mathcal G$ at $q$, assumed to be a section transport (`h𝒯`: its variable-change and coefficient-change operations move the two sections $P,Q$ of a raw Drinfeld pair along the graded maps realising variable change, respectively base change). The hypotheses `hVC` and `hCO` assert that such graded ring homomorphisms exist: for every $A$-algebra $T$, projective Weierstrass curve $W$ and variable change $C$ (respectively every $A$-algebra map $f : T \to T'$) there is a graded homomorphism between the projective model graded rings, dominating the irrelevant ideal, satisfying `IsVariableChangeHom` (respectively `IsCoefficientHom`).
--
--   **The relabelling family.** $\rho$ assigns to each $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ an automorphism of the moduli problem `(rigidDataH1Pow A ℓg M' q hℓ hM hL 𝒢 𝒯).toLevelModuliDatum`, i.e. a $j$-preserving operation on points functorial in the $A$-algebra. The hypothesis `hρ` pins down $\rho$ on raw data over fields: for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, a field $T$ that is an $A$-algebra, raw points $x, x'$ over $T$ and a witness that the discriminant of the curve of the Drinfeld component of $x$ is a unit, if $x'$ and $x$ have the same Weierstrass curve, the same $\Gamma_0$-component `level.1`, if the point of $x'$ given by $(x_P,y_P)$ is $\gamma_{00}$ times the corresponding point of $x$ in the group of affine points of the curve of $x$ over $T$, if $x'$ has $x_Q = x_P$ and $y_Q = y_P$, and if the Drinfeld component of $x'$ is `RawDrinfeldPair.relabel 𝒢` applied to the integral matrix of $\gamma$ and the Drinfeld component of $x$, then $\rho(\gamma)$ carries the class of $x$ to the class of $x'$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There is a point $x_0$ of the moduli problem over $K$ such that the following three assertions hold.
--
--   First, the image of $j(x_0) \in K$ in $L((\mathsf q))$ is [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18), the $q$-fold substitution $\mathsf q \mapsto \mathsf q^{q}$ in the $j$-series.
--
--   Secondly, there exist a variable change $C$ over $L((\mathsf q))$ and a raw point $r$ over $K$ with the following six properties. (i) The class of $r$ is $x_0$. (ii) Writing $(x_\infty, y_\infty)$ for [`ModularCurve.tateToricPoint L q`](def/ModularCurve_KatzLevelPCusps.html#L20) evaluated at the $q$-th power of the unit attached to $\xi$ (a primitive $\ell_g$-th root of unity), the weight-one normalisation $u \cdot (2x_\infty + \tfrac16) = 2y_\infty + x_\infty$ holds for $u = C.u$, together with $C.r = -\tfrac1{12}$, $C.s = -\tfrac12$, $C.t = \tfrac1{24}$ as constant Laurent series. (iii) The curve of $r$, pushed along $K \hookrightarrow L((\mathsf q))$, equals $C \bullet$ [`ModularCurve.tateBase L q`](def/ModularCurve_TateSlots.html#L46). (iv) For every prime factor $p$ of $M'$, every field $F'$, every ring homomorphism $f : L \to F'$ and every primitive $p^{v_p(M')}$-th root of unity $\zeta \in F'$, the $p$-component kernel polynomial of $r$, pushed to $L((\mathsf q))$ and then mapped coefficientwise by $f$, equals `kernelVariableChangeDeg` of $C$ mapped by $f$, of degree `gamma0PowDeg p (M'.factorization p)`, applied to $\prod_a \bigl(X - (\text{first coordinate of } \mathrm{ModularCurve.toricPoint}\ F'\ q\ (\zeta^a))\bigr)$, the product being over $1 \le a \le p^{v_p(M')}/2$ with $p \nmid a$. (v) The quadruple `r.level.2.1`, pushed to $L((\mathsf q))$, equals the $C$-transform of the quadruple with both abscissae equal to $x_\infty$ and both ordinates equal to $y_\infty$. (vi) There are elements $P_x, P_y, Q_x, Q_y$ of $K$ whose Laurent expansions are the four entries $x_P, y_P, x_Q, y_Q$ of the $C$-transform of [`ModularCurve.cuspData L q`](def/ModularCurve_KatzLevelPCusps.html#L71) at the $\ell_g$-th power of the unit attached to $\xi$ (a primitive $q$-th root of unity) with vectors $(1,0)$ and $(0,-1)$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/q)^2$, and the sections $P$ and $Q$ of the Drinfeld component of $r$ pass through $(P_x,P_y)$ and $(Q_x,Q_y)$ respectively in the sense of `IsSectionThrough`.
--
--   Thirdly, for every $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ lying in $\Gamma_0(M')$ and every $L$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ satisfying [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q (ξ ^ ℓg) q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) — that is, for all weights $k$, all modular forms $f, g$ of weight $k$ on the group attached to $\Gamma_H(q^2M')$ with $H = H_1$, all integral $q$-expansions of $f$ and $g$ with that of $g$ having nonzero associated series, every $x \in K$ whose Laurent expansion is the coefficientwise image of the quotient of those series, and every $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\xi^{\ell_g}) = \exp(2\pi i/q)$, the $\iota$-image of $\tau x$ multiplied by the $q$-expansion of $g \mid_k$ `conjElemN q γ⁻¹` equals the $q$-expansion of $f \mid_k$ `conjElemN q γ⁻¹` — the functorial map of the moduli problem along $\tau$, viewed as an $A$-algebra homomorphism, sends $x_0$ to $\rho(\gamma)$ applied to $x_0$.
--
--   This is the identification, in the $H_1$-moduli problem guarded by a $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-point at an auxiliary prime $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$, of the action of the level automorphisms of the function field $K$ on a distinguished Tate point with the relabelling (diamond) action $\rho$ of $\Gamma_0(M')$: same curve, same $\Gamma_0$-data, the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-point multiplied by $\gamma_{00}$, and the Drinfeld $\Gamma(q)$-pair relabelled by $\gamma$. It supplies the Tate point together with its normalisations to the subsequent comparison of the chart with the moduli problem and to the maximality arguments built on it.
